  "narrative": "The library-stratified and co-occurrence-stratified robustness checks strongly support the central SMARCA4-loss claim. For the signed -1 hypotheses, positive estimates indicate stronger composite dependency in SMARCA4-loss cells. In both Avana and non-Avana libraries, H15 and H16 have point estimates around +0.83 to +0.87 with lower bounds well above the 0.15 meaningful threshold, so the SMARCA4-loss effect is not confined to one CRISPR library background. The co-occurrence checks are similarly strong: H21 and H22 show meaningful SMARCA4-loss-associated stronger dependency both within TP53-loss and TP53-intact samples, and H23 and H24 show the same within KEAP1-loss and KEAP1-intact samples. This argues against the possibility that the SMARCA4 effect is merely driven by uneven distribution of TP53 or KEAP1 loss. I accept these stratified comparisons and close them as resolved robustness checks.\n\nThe TP53-loss library checks are mixed in strength. H17 in Avana supports a weaker-dependency association robustly: signed estimate +0.429, lower +0.327. H18 in non-Avana is directionally positive and statistically distinguishable from zero, but the lower bound +0.063 falls well below the 0.15 meaningful threshold; with the smaller non-Avana cells, the stratum is too imprecise to conclude a meaningful effect-size difference in that subgroup. I mark H18 unresolved and defer it rather than let it contradict the main accepted H13 result. The KEAP1-loss checks are strong in both libraries: H19 and H20 have signed estimates +0.81 and +0.98 with lower bounds far above 0.15, supporting the accepted weaker-dependency association and showing no library-specific reversal.\n\nSTK11 loss and MTAP loss do not support the anticipated weaker-dependency direction. H25 and H26 have near-zero signed estimates, with intervals including null and entirely below the claimed meaningful direction threshold. These are rejected and closed as uninformative for this outcome.\n\nBecause H9 is now the central accepted claim with strong internal robustness, I request independent validation for H9. This is not required for acceptance, but it is useful to test whether the SMARCA4-loss association with stronger composite dependency generalizes beyond the current discovery contrasts. I do not request validation for H18 because it is a secondary small-stratum uncertainty; validating the main claim is more efficient. These remain dataset-level descriptive associations, not causal claims.",
  "research_notes": "Current evidence after iteration 2 appraisal: H9 SMARCA4 loss associated with stronger composite dependency remains accepted and is internally robust in Avana/non-Avana libraries and within TP53/KEAP1 co-alteration strata. H13 TP53 loss associated with weaker dependency remains accepted; H18 non-Avana TP53 stratum is directionally positive but unresolved due threshold-crossing CI and small cells. H14 KEAP1 loss associated with weaker dependency remains accepted and robust across Avana and non-Avana libraries. H15/H16/H17/H19/H20/H21/H22/H23/H24 are accepted robustness findings and closed. H25 STK11 and H26 MTAP are rejected and closed. One validation request issued for H9. Next after H9 validation: reassess H9 using independent interval; if confirmed, prioritize H27/H28 technical main effects, subtype robustness H29-H31, and remaining genomic predictors H32-H38. Consider KEAP1/TP53 within SMARCA4 strata if co-occurrence confounding for H13/H14 becomes important. Keep H8 CDKN2A unresolved pending further evidence or reconsideration."
}

