ent cells. The MSI-high robustness results are decisive: MSI-high versus non-MSI-high in SMARCA4-intact samples is +0.8169 [0.6930, 0.9408], in TP53-loss samples +0.8841 [0.4552, 1.3130], and in KEAP1-loss samples +0.8542 [0.6276, 1.0808]. All full intervals are above the threshold, so I accept and close these subgroup associations, while noting the small exposed cell counts in H93 and H94. This strengthens the conclusion that MSI-high is robustly associated with stronger composite dependency and is not confined to a single central-predictor background. The remaining broad marker checks are nonmeaningful: EGFR amplification +0.0919 [-0.0137, 0.1974] and BRAF V600E -0.0488 [-0.1691, 0.0714]. I reject and close both, completing this marker screen. Overall, the dataset-internal map remains: SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high are robustly associated with stronger dependency; TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss are associated with weaker dependency; PTEN loss is conditionally associated with stronger dependency, now including the broader TP53-intact context, but global PTEN remains unresolved and the pattern appears conditional rather than homogeneous. Validation is requested for H62 only because it is the strongest newly accepted less-nested PTEN conditional result; validating near-threshold PTEN contexts would be less likely to clear the very conservative validation threshold and is lower priority. All conclusions are descriptive and dataset-internal, not causal, not single-gene knockout-specific, and not evidence of external generalizability.",
