  "narrative": "No discovery assessments are due at this stage. The current dataset-internal map is fairly mature: SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high are robustly associated with stronger composite dependency, TP53 loss is robustly associated with weaker composite dependency, KEAP1 loss is strongly associated with weaker composite dependency in discovery, and PTEN loss shows conditional stronger-dependency signals mainly in TP53-intact or clean MSI-nonhigh contexts but remains unresolved globally. Rather than continuing broad PTEN subgroup mining, I am registering a focused set of comparisons that test the remaining important boundaries: interaction architecture among the strongest predictors, lineage context after removing the dominant MSI signal, and robustness in genomic-instability contexts. First, I refine the suggestive TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction by restricting to MSI-nonhigh samples. Existing evidence suggested TP53-loss may have a larger weaker-dependency effect when KEAP1 is lost, but the interaction could be influenced by small or MSI-confounded cells. This is a falsifiable test of nonadditive TP53-KEAP1 biology independent of MSI status. Second, I refine the SMARCA4-by-lineage interaction by restricting to MSI-nonhigh samples. The prior result suggested the SMARCA4-loss association may be stronger outside adenocarcinoma relative to adenocarcinoma; the MSI-restricted refinement tests whether that apparent subtype modification persists when the very strong MSI-high effect is removed. Third, I register an MSI-high-by-TP53-loss interaction to test whether MSI-high dependency association is additive or synergistically/attenuated by TP53 status. Existing subgroup estimates are directionally consistent with slightly stronger MSI association in TP53-loss, but that may be noise from small exposed cells. Fourth through sixth, I test whether the three strongest central associations remain meaningful in top-quartile aneuploidy_score cells excluding MSI-high. These contexts are scientifically useful because high chromosomal instability can modify fitness effects of driver losses; the alternative is that the associations are independent of CNA context and robust to genomic instability. Seventh, as a secondary exploratory robustness check, I test SMARCA4-loss association in high non-MSI mutation burden. This tests whether high mutation burden without MSI alters the strongest known dependency predictor. Directions follow the known raw signs: SMARCA4 and MSI-high raw contrasts are expected negative for stronger dependency; TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss raw contrasts are expected positive for weaker dependency. For interactions, the signed claim directions match the proposed hypotheses: TP53-by-KEAP1 direction +1 tests a positive raw interaction with TP53-loss having a larger weaker-dependency effect when KEAP1 is lost; SMARCA4-by-adenocarcinoma direction +1 tests a positive raw interaction meaning adenocarcinoma SMARCA4 effects are less negative than non-adenocarcinoma effects, i.e., stronger dependency association outside adenocarcinoma; MSI-by-TP53-loss direction -1 tests whether MSI-high effects are more negative, or stronger dependency, in TP53-loss than TP53-intact samples. All returned estimates will be interpreted as already signed to these claim directions and compared with the 0.15 composite-unit threshold. These remain descriptive dataset-internal associations, not causal claims, not single-gene knockout specificity for the composite endpoint, and not evidence of generalizability.",
