d at the threshold level. H111 tests TP53-loss by KEAP1-loss interaction restricted to MSI-nonhigh samples; the signed estimate is +0.1382 with interval -0.0565 to +0.3329. The point is below threshold and the interval includes zero, so I reject this registered interaction. This weakens the earlier suggestive H102 TP53-by-KEAP1 interaction, and I move H102 from active to deferred because the MSI-restricted refinement did not clear the threshold. H112 tests SMARCA4-loss by lineage interaction restricted to MSI-nonhigh samples; the signed estimate is +0.2146 with interval +0.0656 to +0.3636. The point exceeds threshold, but the lower bound remains below threshold, and this resembles the earlier unrefined H78 result. I therefore leave H112 unresolved and defer further investigation; I also defer H78 because H112 does not resolve the interaction. H113 tests MSI-high by TP53-loss interaction; the signed estimate is +0.1235 with wide interval -0.3163 to +0.5633. The exposed MSI-high/TP53-loss cell is small (24), and the result is below threshold and imprecise, so I leave it unresolved and deferred rather than closing as a precise null. H95 tests MSI-high by lineage subtype interaction; the signed estimate is -0.1760 with interval -0.4351 to +0.0831. It is not consistently meaningful in the claimed direction and includes zero, so I reject and close it. Overall, the dataset-internal map strengthens: SMARCA4 loss, MSI-high, TP53 loss, and KEAP1 loss effects are robust across genomic-instability contexts; MSI-high remains associated with stronger dependency even within SMARCA4-loss cells; PTEN loss now has a new accepted TP53-intact/MSI-nonhigh conditional association pending independent validation. Interaction evidence remains weak, with only H110 receiving validation because it most directly clarifies the remaining PTEN boundary. All conclusions are descriptive and dataset-internal, not causal, not single-gene knockout specificity for the composite endpoint, and not evidence of external generalizability.",
