  "narrative": "No discovery assessments are currently due. The current dataset-internal map is mature: SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high are robustly associated with stronger composite dependency, TP53 loss is robustly associated with weaker composite dependency, and KEAP1 loss is strongly associated with weaker composite dependency, especially in many conditional contexts. PTEN loss is best supported as a conditional stronger-dependency predictor in TP53-intact and MSI-nonhigh backgrounds, while global PTEN remains unresolved. The newly active comparisons H118, H119, and H120 already test PTEN in TP53-intact/MSI-nonhigh/high aneuploidy and TP53/KEAP1 in high non-MSI mutation burden. I am therefore registering three focused refinements that address remaining boundaries without repeating already tested near-threshold PTEN contexts. First, PTEN loss in TP53-intact, MSI-nonhigh, high mutation_burden samples tests whether the accepted TP53-intact/MSI-nonhigh PTEN association remains meaningful under elevated mutational burden without MSI. The alternative is that PTEN effects are more tightly linked to the broader TP53-intact/MSI-nonhigh state and attenuate once non-MSI mutational load is high. Second, MSI-high in high mutation_burden samples tests whether the very strong MSI-high association persists after conditioning on mutation burden. If MSI-high remains strongly associated with stronger dependency in high mutation burden, that supports an MSI-specific association beyond simple mutation-burden correlation; if it attenuates below the 0.15-unit threshold, mutation burden or correlated MSI-high biology may explain more of the original association. Third, SMARCA4 loss in high ploidy and MSI-nonhigh samples tests the strongest known predictor under an alternative chromosomal-instability measure distinct from aneuploidy_score. This is a robustness test: if SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency persists with elevated ploidy, the association is less likely to be an artifact of the specific aneuploidy metric. Directions follow the known raw contrasts: SMARCA4-loss and MSI-high raw contrasts are expected negative, so claims with direction -1 are signed positive if stronger dependency is observed; PTEN-loss in accepted conditional contexts also has negative raw contrast, so direction -1 tests stronger dependency with PTEN loss. All proposed estimates will be interpreted as already signed to the registered direction and judged against the 0.15 composite dependency-score threshold. These remain descriptive, dataset-internal associations, not causal effects, not single-gene knockout specificity for the composite endpoint, and not evidence of external generalizability. If H121 and H122 clear threshold, PTEN becomes more robust in a genomic-instability/nonMSI background and MSI remains specific after mutation-burden conditioning. If they do not, PTEN and MSI should remain interpreted as conditional or context-limited within this dataset. H123, if clear, further strengthens SMARCA4-loss robustness across chromosomal-instability contexts; if not, SMARCA4 may be somewhat sensitive to ploidy context. For remaining validation priority, global KEAP1 loss remains the most useful active target because it is the only central predictor without independent validation.",
