  "narrative": "I assessed the twelve newly delivered discovery results, all signed to their registered hypothesis directions and judged against the 0.15 composite-dependency-unit absolute threshold. H136 tests MSI-high versus MSI-nonhigh among high-ploidy samples: signed estimate +0.838 (95% CI +0.687 to +0.990), entirely above threshold in the stronger-dependency direction. H137 tests MSI-high versus MSI-nonhigh among high-aneuploidy-score samples: signed estimate +0.822 (95% CI +0.674 to +0.969), also clearly above threshold. I accept and close both. They are conditional associations in this supplied dataset, not causal or external generalization claims, but they strengthen the dataset-internal robustness of the accepted MSI-high stronger-dependency association across chromosomal-instability contexts. The remaining new claims tested effect modification by ploidy, aneuploidy score, or growth rate. H138-H141, H142-H145, and H147 are near-null interactions: point estimates are either below the threshold or in the opposite direction, and their intervals include null. For example, H138 SMARCA4-loss interaction with high ploidy among MSI-nonhigh samples gives +0.139 (CI -0.009 to +0.287), H140 KEAP1-loss interaction gives +0.095 (CI -0.051 to +0.240), H141 MSI-high interaction with high ploidy gives +0.112 (CI -0.122 to +0.346), and H147 MSI-high growth-rate interaction gives +0.005 (CI -0.260 to +0.270). These do not support scientifically meaningful context-specific amplification in the registered direction; the evidence is more compatible with additive stability of the central associations. I reject and close H138-H145 and H147. H146 is the only borderline exception: the signed estimate for the SMARCA4-loss interaction with high growth rate is -0.156 (CI -0.308 to -0.003), which is opposite to the registered stronger-dependency interaction direction and corresponds to a raw positive interaction, suggesting possible attenuation of the SMARCA4-loss association in faster-growing MSI-nonhigh samples. However, because the result is near the practical threshold, directionally opposite to the pre-specified claim, part of a large multiplicity-heavy interaction panel, and no independent validation remains available, I do not promote it as a new supported boundary condition. Critical-review notes: subgroup cutpoints are measured baseline biological or technical variables rather than outcome-derived, so direct outcome leakage is not apparent, but ploidy, aneuploidy, and MSI status are correlated, and some growth-rate cells exclude missing observations. Multiple testing is a concern; the accepted main-effects are accepted because their lower bounds are far above the effect-size threshold, whereas the interactions are rejected because they lack precise, threshold-clearing effects in the claimed direction. No validation request is made because the remaining new voluntary validation budget is zero. No new proposals are registered, since further near-threshold subgroup mining is likely to create false precision rather than scientifically decisive information.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 appraise complete. New results close the high-chromosomal-instability and proliferation-context follow-up. MSI-high remains strongly associated with stronger composite dependency in high-ploidy and high-aneuploidy contexts (H136/H137 accepted/closed). All registered ploidy, aneuploidy, and growth-rate effect-modification tests failed to establish robust amplification in the claimed direction (H138-H145, H147 rejected/closed). H146 shows a borderline opposite-direction interaction: SMARCA4-loss stronger-dependency association may be attenuated in high growth-rate MSI-nonhigh samples, but the magnitude is only near threshold, the direction was not pre-specified, multiplicity is high, and validation budget is exhausted; it is not promoted. Mature dataset-internal map: SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high -> stronger dependency; TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency; PTEN loss conditionally associated with stronger dependency in TP53-intact/MSI-nonhigh or otherwise cleaner backgrounds. Independent validation budget exhausted. Future analysis should avoid further near-boundary subgroup mining unless a genuinely new comparison, validation, or external dataset is available."
