  "narrative": "All 12 newly delivered discovery contrasts are signed to their registered directions, and every comparison cell satisfies the minimum 20-observation requirement. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate indicates stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 claims, it indicates weaker dependency. In the largest screen batch (batch_a), the central predictors remain strongly associated in the same directions as the accepted overall pattern: SMARCA4 loss stronger dependency (H193/R195 +0.862; 95% CI +0.737 to +0.988), KEAP1 loss weaker dependency (H194/R196 +0.733; +0.595 to +0.871), TP53 loss weaker dependency (H195/R197 +0.456; +0.271 to +0.640), and MSI-high stronger dependency (H196/R198 +0.805; +0.578 to +1.032). All point estimates exceed the 0.15-unit threshold with intervals comfortably supporting the claimed direction, so I accept and close these batch-robustness contrasts. In the Ky CRISPR library subset, SMARCA4 loss (H197/R199 +0.830; +0.705 to +0.956), KEAP1 loss (H198/R200 +0.942; +0.806 to +1.078), and MSI-high (H200/R202 +0.981; +0.757 to +1.206) remain threshold-clear and are accepted. However, TP53 loss in the Ky subset (H199/R201) has a positive signed estimate (+0.226) but a 95% CI from +0.016 to +0.436, which is compatible with effects below the 0.15-unit scientific threshold. This is notably attenuated relative to the accepted global TP53 association and is not precise enough to conclude a scientifically meaningful threshold-clear effect. I therefore leave it unresolved but deferred rather than reject it, because the evidence supports the direction but not the threshold with confidence; it may reflect a library-context attenuation, chance, or limited precision. In low-ploidy MSI-nonhigh samples, SMARCA4 loss (H201/R203 +0.794; +0.661 to +0.926), KEAP1 loss (H202/R204 +0.839; +0.718 to +0.960), and TP53 loss (H203/R205 +0.380; +0.195 to +0.564) all clear the threshold and are accepted, reducing concern that the central loss-marker associations are driven only by highly aneuploid or MSI-high backgrounds. Finally, MSI-high association remains strong among samples lacking complete integrated RNA/DNA/CRISPR QC (H204/R206 +0.802; +0.541 to +1.062), and is accepted. These are pre-specified robustness contrasts, but the overall multiplicity burden in the full analysis remains substantial; the accepted results are unusually large and consistent, so simple threshold assessment is reasonable, while the single borderline TP53-Ky result is appropriately held unresolved. The subsets are technical or genomic strata, so measured stratification does not prove confounding is absent, and selection/collider effects cannot be ruled out. These conclusions remain dataset-internal associations on a composite dependency endpoint, not causal effects, single-gene essentiality claims, or evidence of external generalizability. Validation budget is exhausted, so no new validation request is made.",
