brary, matched omics, media serum percentage, and sex are rejected; and most interaction analyses do not establish meaningful effect modification. Major unresolved uncertainty remains about the global PTEN-loss boundary, possible Ky-library attenuation of the TP53-loss association (H199 and pending H205), and the pending interaction/boundary analyses H45/H46 and H205-H214. The most informative next analysis is to evaluate those pending interaction and boundary claims against the 0.15 threshold and interval precision, especially H205-H213 and H214; if they remain imprecise or fail to clear the threshold, they should be closed as inconclusive. If external follow-up becomes possible, the highest-value next step is replication of the four central associations and PTEN context-dependence using orthogonal dependency endpoints and adjustment for lineage, mutation burden or ploidy, screen quality, Cas9 activity, QC completeness, and library context. All conclusions remain observational dataset-internal associations and do not establish causality, mechanistic single-gene essentiality, clinical transportability, or external generalizability.",
